5b. B1, THE COMBINATION RULE (SESSION, from what the rating is)
   The 2.8 A is a thermal rating (MAKER p.1: 4000 h at 125 C with the rated ripple applied). Two harmonics at f1 and f2 heat as
   i^2: their cross term beats at |f1 - f2|, and the can's temperature follows a beat slower than its thermal time constant and
   averages a faster one. The sheet prints no time constant; 1 s is taken as its shortest (ASSUMPTION), so a beat under
   1 / (2 pi tau) = 0.16 Hz heats as coherent at the worst relative phase, and a faster one in mean square. Both converters'
   frequencies are continuous bands and the two oscillators are independent, so EXACT coincidence p fsw = q fch is permitted:
   the worst case sits on it, whatever the time constant. The rule: on every permitted ratio p / q with p <= 20, fsw along the
   line every 0.5 kHz inside both bands, every VIN and VBAT, the coincident pairs (p m, q m) add with one common time shift
   maximised, the rest in mean square; ratios with p > 20 are bounded by Cauchy-Schwarz on the harmonics above 20 (tail_bound)
   the check's counterexample (eight cans, 396 uF, the low-ESR can 6 mOhm and 1.5 nH, its siblings 12.5 mOhm and 2.0 nH, ceramics
     4 uF, 2 mOhm, 1.5 nH; VIN 9 V, VBAT 10 V, 7.2621 A): 2.7387 A in mean square at 198.8 / 816 kHz (the check: 2.738774); 2.8547 A at
     204 / 816 kHz, the front end's 4th harmonic on the charger's fundamental (the check: 2.854635); 2.8138 A at 204 / 408 kHz
     (the check: 2.813686). The rule puts both inside: exact coincidences, coherent at the worst phase; that bank fails

6. THE RE-SIZE (SESSION): A BALLAST RESISTOR IN EVERY CAN'S BRANCH, THE SIX CANS KEPT
   Why a ballast, and why it is the smallest robust change:
   - the sheet prints the cans' ESR maxima only (20 mOhm at +20 C, p.2; 200 % of it after endurance and 300 mOhm at -40 C after
     endurance, p.1); without a resistance floor (above)
     no count of cans is robust, and an unballasted can with a low ESR takes its siblings' share, so every can needs one;
   - a ballast puts a printed floor in every branch (HoJLR2512, +-1 %, +-50 ppm/K, 3 W; MAKER, v2/vendor/passives/milliohm-hojlr2512-series.pdf p.2) and damps the node;
   - with it the six drawn cans serve: no can is added, so the node's capacitance, the soft start, the bleed and the restart
     ring stay as drawn (section 8); adding cans adds the same resistors and more parts
   The model: every can independent, C 0.56 to 1.56 x 330 uF (p.1 +-20 % and +-30 % over endurance), ESR 0 to 300 mOhm with no
     floor (p.2's 20 mOhm at +20 C, 200 % after endurance (p.1), and size G's 300 mOhm after endurance at -40 C (p.1), taken to bound
     the in-use minimum of -20 C: INFERRED, the hybrid's ESR rising as it cools; the grid every 5 mOhm to 40 mOhm, then 60, 100, 150, 200, 300 mOhm),
     branch L 2.0 to 5.0 nH (the can's 1.5 to 3.5 nH INFERRED, the ballast's 0.5 to 1.5 nH INFERRED); the ballast at 1.38 % low
     on the target and high on its siblings (1 % and 50 ppm/K over 75 K); the siblings carry the layout's 0.5 nH and 0.5 mOhm;
     the target and its siblings searched independently, the siblings alike (a split into two groups checked in 6b)
